Should `422` be returned instead of `400` for malformed Json?

Error `422` is returned in case of a malformed request body, like the following:
```
POST http://localhost:8000/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Sample Item",
  "sa
}
```
Error Response:
```
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Content
date: Sun, 23 Nov 2025 17:01:45 GMT
server: uvicorn
content-length: 138
content-type: application/json

{
  "detail": [
    {
      "type": "json_invalid",
      "loc": [
        "body",
        32
      ],
      "msg": "JSON decode error",
      "input": {},
      "ctx": {
        "error": "Invalid control character at"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
Doesn't it make sense to return `400` in case of malformed JSON? [RFC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4918#section-11.2) suggests 422 for validation failures

